PART 1 — Correct the analysis sheet first
Three defects were found in Sheet5. Two of them inflate costs. None are visible from glancing at the sheet — they only show up when individual rows are cross-checked against the portal.

1.1 Licence cost is being counted twice ❗ Critical
What is happening. In Sheet1, the SQL licence charge appears with ServiceTier SQL DB Single/Elastic Pool GP-SQL Licen and Meter vCore. The compute formula (column W) matches any Meter containing vCore, so it picks up the licence line. The licence formula (column Y) then picks it up again by ServiceTier. Every LicenseIncluded database is overstated by exactly its licence cost.

Evidence — three independent cross-checks against the portal:

Database	Sheet5 total (AB)	Licence (Y)	AB − Y	Portal figure	Match
vos-iaz-sql-dev-datasyncs / vosiazsqldbdevndrdatasyncR2	£14,752.31	£3,766.98	£10,985.33	£10,985.33	✅
ctp-iaz-sql-sit-revaluation / ctpiazsqldbsitrevaluation	£847.05	£188.35	£658.70	£658.70	✅
ctp-iaz-sql-dev-revaluation / ctpiazsqldbdevrevaluation	£479.12	£94.17	£384.94	£384.94	✅
In all three cases, removing the licence figure once lands exactly on the portal total. The defect is confirmed.

Impact. Your single largest finding currently shows as £177,027 per year. The true figure is ~£131,824 per year. Presenting the inflated figure would be discovered on the first cross-check and would undermine every other number in the report.

1.2 A second, smaller double-count on backups
Some backup meters are named Backup - LRS Data Stored. That text contains both Data Stored (matched by the storage formula) and Backup (matched by the backup formula). Those rows are counted twice.

This is small in value — for example about £26 on bstiazsqldbsitdap — but it is the same class of error and is fixed by the same change.

1.3 The fix — classify each cost row exactly once
Rather than patching the SUMIFS filters, classify every row of Sheet1 into one category. Each row then gets counted in exactly one place, and double-counting becomes impossible.

Step 1. In Sheet1, insert a new column (K), header category, and in K2:

=IF(ISNUMBER(SEARCH("Defender",E2)),"Defender",
 IF(ISNUMBER(SEARCH("Licen",F2)),"Licence",
 IF(OR(ISNUMBER(SEARCH("Backup",F2)),ISNUMBER(SEARCH("Backup",G2))),"Backup",
 IF(ISNUMBER(SEARCH("Data Stored",G2)),"Storage",
 IF(OR(ISNUMBER(SEARCH("vCore",G2)),ISNUMBER(SEARCH("DTU",G2))),"Compute","Other")))))
The order of the tests matters. Licence is tested before Compute because the licence meter is named vCore. Backup is tested before Storage because backup meters contain Data Stored.

Assumes Sheet1 columns: E = ServiceName, F = ServiceTier, G = Meter, H = Cost (GBP), J = MatchKey. Adjust if yours differ.

Fill down, then convert column K to values (copy → paste special → values) to keep the workbook responsive.

Step 2. Replace Sheet5 columns W–AA:

Col	Header	Formula (row 2)
W	computeCost	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2,Sheet1!$K:$K,"Compute")
X	storageCost	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2,Sheet1!$K:$K,"Storage")
Y	licenceCost	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2,Sheet1!$K:$K,"Licence")
Z	backupCost	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2,Sheet1!$K:$K,"Backup")
AA	otherCost	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2,Sheet1!$K:$K,"Other")
AB	totalCost	=SUM($W2:$AA2)
AC	annualised	=$AB2*12
AD	checkTotal	=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$H:$H,Sheet1!$J:$J,$A2)
AE	reconciles?	=IF(ABS($AB2-$AD2)<0.01,"OK","MISMATCH")
Filter AE for MISMATCH. There should be none. If there are, a row in Sheet1 has fallen outside the categories — inspect it.

Why Defender is now removed from the per-database view. Defender for SQL is billed at server level. Server-level charges have a blank MatchKey, so they never match a database row. Your current defenderCost column shows £0 for every database — not because Defender is free, but because it cannot be attributed this way. Total it separately (Finding 11).

1.4 Hyperscale maximum size produces nonsense values
For the three Hyperscale databases, Resource Graph returns maxSizeBytes = -1 meaning "not applicable". The current formula divides it anyway, producing -9.31323E-10 in column R, and from there huge negative figures in U and V (for example −1,724.38 GB reclaimable and a percentage in the trillions).

Fix — column R:

=IF($A2="","",IF(Sheet3!J2<=0,"",Sheet3!J2/1073741824))
This was an error in the formula I originally supplied. Hyperscale is billed on allocated storage, not maximum, so a blank is correct here.

1.5 Missing storage metrics are being read as zero
When a database has no metric data — Inaccessible, or paused for the whole sampling window — Sheet4 has a blank cell. VLOOKUP returns a blank as 0. The sheet then treats "we don't know" as "zero GB allocated", and column V reports the entire maximum size as reclaimable.

Example: bstdevmigration shows 3,274 GB reclaimable. That figure is not real — we simply have no allocation data for it.

Fix — columns S, T, U, V:

Col	Formula
S usedGB	=IFERROR(IF(INDEX(Sheet4!$D:$D,MATCH($A2,Sheet4!$C:$C,0))="","",INDEX(Sheet4!$D:$D,MATCH($A2,Sheet4!$C:$C,0))),"")
T allocatedGB	=IFERROR(IF(INDEX(Sheet4!$E:$E,MATCH($A2,Sheet4!$C:$C,0))="","",INDEX(Sheet4!$E:$E,MATCH($A2,Sheet4!$C:$C,0))),"")
U usedPctOfMax	=IF(OR($R2="",$S2=""),"",$S2/$R2)
V reclaimableGB	=IF(OR($R2="",$T2=""),"",$R2-$T2)
After this, blanks mean "unknown" and zeros mean zero. Treat every blank in V as needs measuring, not as a saving.

1.6 Reconcile against the whole
Once 1.3–1.5 are done, confirm the sheet accounts for all SQL database spend.

In any spare cell:

Attributed to databases:   =SUM(Sheet5!AB2:AB97)
Total SQL in Sheet1:       =SUMIFS(Sheet1!H:H, Sheet1!E:E, "SQL Database")
Unattributed:              = difference of the two
The unattributed remainder is expected to be small and should consist of server-level charges (long-term retention backups on servers, including droppedserver) and any database that existed during the month but has since been deleted. If it is large, investigate before continuing — it means databases are missing from Sheet3.

