FINDING 4 — High minimum vCore floors on Serverless databases
Priority: 3. Settings change only. No migration, no data movement. Likely a template-level pattern — potentially fixable once, everywhere.

What was observed
A group of Serverless databases carry minCapacity of 10 or 20 vCores. A Serverless database bills at least its minimum every second it is online, whether or not it is doing work.

Database	Env	Min	Max	Auto-pause	Monthly
bstsitlaserver / bstsitlarep	SIT	20	40	180 min	£4,867.15
vos-iaz-sql-dev-datamig / vosiazsqldbdevndrdatamig	DEV	10	80	60	£1,397.46
bstdatadev / bstdevpoststaging	DEV	10	80	60	£816.81
vos-iaz-sql-dev-datasyncs / vosiazsqldbdevndrdatasync	DEV	10	80	60	£696.29
vos-iaz-sql-sit-datamig / vosiazsqldbsitndrdatamig	SIT	20	40	60	£922.84
vos-iaz-sql-uat-datasyncs / vosiazsqldbuatndrdatasync	UAT	20	40	60	£792.35
vos-iaz-sql-sit-datasyncs / vosiazsqldbsitndrdatasync	SIT	20	40	60	£690.67
bstdatadev / bstdevmigration	DEV	10	80	120	£503.19
vos-iaz-sql-uat-datamig / vosiazsqldbuatndrdatamig	UAT	20	40	60	£220.92
vos-iaz-sql-mo-datamig / vosiazsqldbmondrdatamig	MO	20	40	60	£126.36
The pattern
Look at the ndr datasync and datamig databases across DEV, SIT, UAT and MO. They share near-identical settings — min 10 or 20, max 40 or 80, 60-minute delay. Identical configuration across environments almost always means the values are set in a shared template or parameter file.

That is significant: if confirmed, correcting one Bicep parameter file fixes the whole family and prevents the problem recurring in new environments.

Why it costs money — bstsitlarep as the worked example
bstsitlarep costs £4,867/month, second only to Finding 3.

Min 20 vCores means that for every hour it is online, it bills at least 20 vCores' worth, even at 0% CPU.
Auto-pause delay of 180 minutes means it must receive zero activity for three unbroken hours before it pauses. Any monitoring agent, health probe, connection pool or scheduled job that touches it inside three hours resets the clock. In a working environment that gap rarely occurs, so in practice it seldom pauses.
Storage: max 1,280 GB, but only 351 GB allocated and 38 GB used. 929 GB is paid for and empty.
The two settings together mean the database has the price profile of a large Provisioned server while being configured as Serverless.

Recommendation
Setting	Current	Recommended starting point
Min vCores	10–20	0.5
Auto-pause delay	60–180 min	60 min
Max vCores	40–80	Set from observed peak — typically 2× the 30-day maximum app_cpu_billed
Max storage	1,280 GB (bstsitlarep)	~450 GB (allocated 351 + headroom)
Saving
Illustrative, pending utilisation data. The minimum floor is the controllable component. For bstsitlarep alone, reducing min from 20 to 0.5 removes almost the entire guaranteed floor, and a working 60-minute pause delay could cut compute by a large fraction if the environment is idle overnight and at weekends.

A realistic conservative planning figure for the whole group is 30–50% of their combined compute cost, to be confirmed with app_cpu_billed data after a one-week trial on one database.

Storage on bstsitlarep (calculated): 929 GB × £0.1095 = ~£102/month, with no performance impact.

Risk
Cold start. When a paused Serverless database receives a connection, it takes up to around a minute to resume, and the first connection may fail. Applications need retry logic. This is normally acceptable in DEV and SIT but must be confirmed with each owning team — particularly for anything with scheduled data syncs, where a failed first connection could break a job.

Steps
Pull 30 days of app_cpu_billed (Sum), app_cpu_percent (Avg and Max) and connection_successful for each database.
Check the Bicep repository for where minCapacity and autoPauseDelay are set.
Trial on one database first — bstsitlarep is the obvious candidate given its size. Change settings, monitor for one week, confirm no job failures.
If successful, change the template and roll through environments in pipeline order.
