⚠️ Correction to earlier analysis
An earlier version of this analysis stated these databases were "essentially zero in size". That was wrong — it misread the -1 placeholder Hyperscale returns for maximum size. The storage metrics now show their real sizes: 400 GB to 1.7 TB.

Why it costs money
Hyperscale is designed for very large databases (up to 128 TB) and rapid scale-out. Its Serverless variant cannot auto-pause — that capability exists only in General Purpose. With a minimum of 5 vCores, each database bills at least 5 vCores continuously, forever.

The key question — do these need Hyperscale?
All three would fit within General Purpose limits (up to 4 TB). Hyperscale is justified only by size well beyond that, very high I/O throughput, or a genuine need for fast restores or read-scale replicas. At 400 GB–1.7 TB in non-production, none of those is evident from the data.

Recommendation
Confirm with the owning team (these are bst … dap — data analytics platform) why Hyperscale was chosen. There may be a performance reason not visible in configuration.
If none: move to General Purpose Serverless, min 0.5, auto-pause 60.
Constraint
Azure supports reverse migration from Hyperscale to General Purpose only within 45 days of the original move to Hyperscale. These were created in mid-2024, so that window has long closed. Moving them requires copying the data into a new General Purpose database (export/import or a database copy) and repointing connections. Plan this as a proper migration, not a setting change.

Saving
Illustrative. Depends on how often the workload is idle. If the environments are unused overnight and at weekends, General Purpose Serverless with auto-pause could reduce compute substantially. Obtain 30 days of app_cpu_billed before committing to a figure.

