FINDING 10 — Geo-redundant backups in non-production
Priority: 9. Hygiene rather than saving.

What was observed
Many databases have backupRedundancy = Geo, meaning backups are replicated to a second Azure region. This protects against a regional outage — a reasonable choice for production, harder to justify for development and test.

Honest assessment of value
The backup cost column (Z) is close to zero for almost every database. Point-in-time restore backup storage is free up to the database's maximum size in the vCore model, and included in DTU pricing. The money involved is small.

Recommendation
Change non-production databases to Local redundancy as good practice, and set it as the default in templates. Present it as a policy alignment item, not a saving.

Note: changing redundancy only affects backups taken afterwards.

FINDING 11 — Defender for SQL on every non-production server
Priority: 10. Security policy decision, not a cost decision.

What was observed
Microsoft Defender for SQL is billed per server per month (~£9.48 per server in the observed data). Because it is server-level, it does not appear in the per-database analysis.

Total it directly from Sheet1:

=SUMIFS(Sheet1!H:H, Sheet1!E:E, "*Defender*")
Recommendation
Raise with the security owner: is Defender for SQL required on every non-production server, or only those holding real or sensitive data?

⚠️ This is not yours to decide. Present the cost and let security own the answer. Disabling a security control to save money, without that decision being made by the right people, is a poor trade.

PART 5 — Before changing any database
These checks are mandatory for every database you propose to change. They go in Sheet5 columns AJ–AP.

5.1 Reservation check
A reservation is a one- or three-year prepayment for a quantity of vCores. Your data includes a "Purchase" charge of ~£1,253 — evidence that at least one reservation exists.

The risk: if you scale down a database whose capacity is covered by a reservation, the reservation keeps being paid but no longer has anything to apply to. The saving evaporates.

Check: Portal → Reservations → review scope, size, family (Gen5 General Purpose), term and utilisation.

5.2 Infrastructure-as-code check
If a database is deployed by Bicep through Azure DevOps, a change made in the portal will be reverted on the next pipeline run.

Check: search the repositories for the database name. If found, the change goes into the template or parameter file and through the pipeline — not the portal.

This is especially relevant for Finding 4, where the uniform settings suggest a shared template.

5.3 Ownership
Every change needs a named owner's agreement, recorded in the ticket. Findings 1 and 6 are CT-owned. The ndr databases appear to be NDR-owned — confirm. The bst … dap databases belong to the data analytics platform team.

5.4 Utilisation evidence
For any compute change, have 30 days of metrics before and after:

Model	Metrics
vCore / Serverless	cpu_percent, app_cpu_billed, app_cpu_percent, app_memory_percent
DTU	dtu_consumption_percent
All	connection_successful
Take both Average and Maximum. Average alone hides spikes; maximum alone hides how idle a database is.

5.5 Change control
Non-production changes still affect testers and pipelines. Follow the programme's change process and give the owning teams notice.

