PART 8 — Best practice going forward
These are the recommendations that stop the same waste recurring. They are arguably more valuable than the one-off savings.

8.1 Standard configuration for non-production
Set these as template defaults, so every new database starts cheap and has to justify being expensive:

Setting	Non-prod default
Compute model	General Purpose Serverless
Min vCores	0.5
Auto-pause	Enabled, 60 minutes
Max vCores	Sized to workload, not maximum available
Backup redundancy	Local
Max storage	Expected size + 30%, reviewed quarterly
Licence	BasePrice where Hybrid Benefit is available
Provisioned, Hyperscale and DTU tiers should require a written justification.

8.2 Enforce through Azure Policy
Configuration drifts unless it's enforced. Azure Policy can:

Audit Serverless databases with auto-pause disabled
Audit non-production databases using Hyperscale or Provisioned tiers
Audit geo-redundant backups outside production
Require owner and expiry tags on every database
Start with Audit effect, not Deny — let teams see the gap before blocking deployments.

8.3 Mandatory tagging
Require on every SQL database:

Tag	Purpose
owner	Who to ask
environment	Stops guessing from names
purpose	What it's for
expiry	When it should be reviewed or removed
The Finding 7 databases would have been flagged automatically years ago with an expiry tag.

8.4 Budgets and alerts
Create a Cost Management budget on the C190VOA subscription with alerts at 80%, 100% and 120%. The subscription currently shows Budget: None.

Also recommend anomaly alerts — Cost Management flagged a "daily run rate 115%" insight during this analysis that went unnoticed.

8.5 Health alerting
Eight databases sat Inaccessible and undetected (Finding 1). Alert on:

SQL database state changing to Inaccessible
Key Vault Unauthorized / Forbidden key operations for any vault holding a TDE protector
Note that key-expiry monitoring alone would not have caught Finding 1 — the key was valid until 2028. The failure was access, not expiry.

8.6 Decommission as a deliverable
Every migration or temporary environment should have decommission written into its plan as a milestone with a named owner. Findings 1, 6 and 7 all stem from environments that outlived their purpose.

8.7 Quarterly review
Repeat this analysis quarterly. The workbook built for this ticket is reusable — refresh the three exports, and Sheet5 recalculates.

8.8 Use Azure Advisor
Azure Advisor produces SQL right-sizing recommendations automatically. Review its Cost tab monthly and use it to corroborate findings like these.

